From 5ad1011d5adba69b846c1758e2fda78ec4cd912b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: telegdyd <telegdyd93@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 01:06:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] jasmine-j0058: the MDP scales the fitted frame (VIG0 QSEED3)
 from the GPU pool, no CPU composeFit; honest IOMobileFramebufferSwapWait (a
 swap is done once its flip latched)

composeFit (CPU nearest-sample scale 1125x2436 -> 996x2160) cost 8.2 ms of a 49 ms
moving frame on CoreAnimation's display thread (j0057 profile). Now:

- VIG0's QSEED3 scaler (stock DT qcom,mdss-vig-scaler-off 0xa00) is programmed as
  msm-4.4 mdss_mdp_qseed3_setup does for RGB without a LUT: bilinear, 21-bit phase
  steps src/dst, preload 4x3, pixel extension = the source size; SSPP source
  1125x2436, output 996x2160 at x 40 (sweet_fit_x/w unchanged, so touch maps as
  before). ABL's register values are kept and restored whenever VIG0 scans a
  panel-size buffer again (flip buffers, console, panic). sweetfb_scale=0: off.
- With the scaler on, backboardd's screen-sized IOSurfaces come from the GPU pool
  (below 4 GiB, contiguous, write-combined) as topaz's gpu=1 did, so a one-layer
  frame is scanned in place (sync path, no latch wait in SwapEnd); other frames
  still go through composeFit. gpu_fbpool=0 turns the pool frames off. SweetSurface
  takes the pool under SweetGPU's pool lock (sweet_gpu_pool_take/give).
- kSweetFBSwapWait (IOMobileFramebufferSwapWait: swap ID, timeout with bit 31 for
  CoreAnimation's polls) returned "done" at once. CoreAnimation's
  IOMFBDisplay::can_update_ frees a queued swap's surface only when SwapWait says
  done, so it drew into the frame still on the panel (j0056's held draws). A swap
  is now done once the MDP latched its flip (CTL_FLUSH clear, or after 50 ms); a
  frame scanned in place stays held until the one after it latched. sweetfb_swapwait=0:
  the old behaviour (topaz default).
- Counters every 600 synced frames: scaled by the MDP / copied, SwapWait calls,
  polls that found the swap not done, time in them.
- sweet_osm command port: the MDP block (0x0c901000-0x0c90a000) readable and
  writable for live display register work.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
